00:18:00.175 --> 00:18:03.295
watershed overlay zone map.

00:18:03.695 --> 00:18:06.495
Appendix three was going to be

00:18:06.895 --> 00:18:08.975
the nutrient allocation worksheet.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:13.420
And what I would like to do is,

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:20.940
my intention is not to have the nutrient allocation worksheet be an actual part of the regulations.

00:18:21.865 --> 00:18:37.620
That is just a tool that we have available to us to help us implement the regulations and it's going to be something that Jim as a zoning agent is using. So I got rid of the reference to appendix three that talked about it. And that gives us the

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:39.220
ability to,

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:43.460
as we see process improvements with using that worksheet,

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:48.980
I can change those without having to go back to public hearing because it's helping us

00:18:49.885 --> 00:18:52.525
easily implement the regulations.

00:18:52.605 --> 00:18:55.085
Doesn't have anything to do with actually

00:18:55.245 --> 00:19:01.965
defining the regulations or anything like that. So there'll be an appendix one and appendix two and that's all there is there.

00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:09.850
Okay,

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:14.570
yep, so now we're down to and we're still really just

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:15.930
providing

00:19:17.325 --> 00:19:19.965
the basic framework for what these

00:19:20.365 --> 00:19:23.965
regulations are all about. In 7.6.4,

00:19:24.845 --> 00:19:25.805
I added

00:19:27.005 --> 00:19:38.180
some words in here based on our discussion at the last meeting. When an undeveloped lot is developed, it would be very difficult for the property owner to reduce nutrient allocation runoff

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:40.340
to achieve the required reduction

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:43.140
required by the nutrient allocation worksheet.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:50.425
In this case, at a minimum, the property owner will mitigate stormwater runoff by capturing all roof and driveway runoff

00:19:50.985 --> 00:19:54.105
in a dry well or detention basin.

00:19:55.785 --> 00:19:56.425
And

00:19:57.545 --> 00:19:59.545
I wrestled with myself

00:20:00.585 --> 00:20:02.025
on this a little bit.

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:08.050
Do I need to keep the words

00:20:08.690 --> 00:20:14.610
driveway or dry well or detention basin or just leave that up to Jim? Jim, you can

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:16.210
weigh in on that a little bit.

00:20:20.245 --> 00:20:22.245
We also talked about giving

00:20:22.245 --> 00:20:23.525
some allowances

00:20:23.525 --> 00:20:24.165
for

00:20:24.485 --> 00:20:25.845
roof, driveway,

00:20:25.925 --> 00:20:26.885
and lawn.

00:20:27.445 --> 00:20:28.565
And I went through

00:20:30.885 --> 00:20:32.165
a case study

00:20:32.325 --> 00:20:34.645
on

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:35.650
just a basic lot.

00:20:36.770 --> 00:20:37.970
I said, well,

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:42.050
just doing the roof and the driveway isn't

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:43.810
going to be enough

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:47.890
because most of the runoff into the lake comes from the lawn.

00:20:49.275 --> 00:20:53.915
It runs off doesn't absorb in the lawn and that sort of thing. So I didn't like

00:20:54.555 --> 00:20:56.555
Scott's recommendation last time.

00:20:57.195 --> 00:21:01.035
And then we went to the last inland wetlands meeting, we had

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:02.860
a potential

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:06.380
property owner. I think they're going to buy one of the lots that's

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:08.060
for sale on Lake Road.

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:11.580
And they were going to the Wetlands Commission with their plan.

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:18.325
So I mentioned to them it'd be nice if they captured all the roof and driveway runoff.

00:21:19.205 --> 00:21:20.405
And they said, well, the

00:21:21.445 --> 00:21:26.245
backside of the roof runs towards the road away from the lake and so does the driveway.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.400
And so they weren't enamored with just doing this much.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:33.040
I think the

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:37.680
thought will grow on them. It just caught them by surprise. But what they did do

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:41.920
is they had a very small lawn on that two acre lot.

00:21:43.225 --> 00:21:44.105
And so

00:21:44.345 --> 00:21:46.105
in keeping with spirit

00:21:46.505 --> 00:21:47.865
of the regulations,

00:21:47.865 --> 00:21:49.625
I thought they did a great job there.

00:21:50.185 --> 00:21:51.225
I asked Jim,

00:21:51.465 --> 00:21:54.985
is the guy who's going to have to go work with the property owners and enforce this,

00:21:55.710 --> 00:21:57.150
which would you prefer?

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:02.350
And he said he's happy with just mitigating the

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:05.150
roof and driveway runoff

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:10.270
and he is not a big fan of big yards and

00:22:09.605 --> 00:22:15.525
the couple remaining lots on the lake, I think are well suited to have very small yards around the house here.

00:22:16.325 --> 00:22:18.805
So I will go with Scott's recommendation,

00:22:18.805 --> 00:22:19.445
barring

00:22:20.165 --> 00:22:22.005
any pushback from anybody here.

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:26.400
>> I

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.840
do have one comment. Last

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:31.360
sentence there in paragraph.

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:33.360
We use the term drywall

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:38.960
or detention basin. I'm wondering if we couldn't just use storage capacity.

00:22:40.135 --> 00:22:45.095
Two reasons. One is it would leave the applicant open to use any other

00:22:46.055 --> 00:22:46.855
means,

00:22:47.815 --> 00:22:55.500
meaning like a rain garden or whatever. Yep. Which is also allowed elsewhere. And dry wealth to me is not a very clear word.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:57.820
I don't think it's clearly defined.

00:22:58.540 --> 00:22:59.340
It's used

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:01.740
differently by different folks

00:23:02.300 --> 00:23:03.500
or it has been over the years.

00:23:05.125 --> 00:23:07.845
I don't think it has a real specific

00:23:08.485 --> 00:23:09.285
definition

00:23:09.285 --> 00:23:10.485
from an engineering

00:23:11.525 --> 00:23:18.270
>> point of view. So if I, you said storage capacity? That's what I'm thinking, yeah. Okay, and so there it is.

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:22.110
Water storage capacity,

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:24.190
perhaps.

00:23:29.470 --> 00:23:34.435
>> Okay, so you like that? Yes, I do. Okay, very good. So that's what

00:23:35.235 --> 00:23:37.235
that will be. Thank you.

00:23:39.875 --> 00:23:41.955
In the next paragraph I just added,

00:23:42.115 --> 00:23:45.155
all that does is that references us down to

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:51.830
a section later on where it talks about if you can't comply with the nutrient allocation worksheet,

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:56.150
you can submit a special permit and take that to the Planning and Zoning Commission.

00:23:56.550 --> 00:23:59.750
So I put that in here because it seemed to be

00:24:01.615 --> 00:24:02.815
a good spot

00:24:04.095 --> 00:24:09.775
to get that thought in our instructions as we go through there. So that's that and

00:24:10.895 --> 00:24:12.735
it doesn't really change the content any.

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:16.710
Leanne, are you able to see us yet

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:18.870
or are you still flying blind?

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:26.390
>> No, I've done this at least eight times and I'm putting in the passcode. I've checked your email to us today. I've checked the one on the town website

00:24:27.315 --> 00:24:28.995
and it just says it's wrong.

00:24:31.955 --> 00:24:34.515
It's 331889.

00:24:35.395 --> 00:24:36.915
>> 331889.

00:24:36.915 --> 00:24:37.715
Yep, that's the passcode.

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:42.460
At least that's the one I used to get in.

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:50.140
>> I don't know what else to do unless I go out and smoke again.

00:24:52.300 --> 00:24:53.580
I just try the phone, Leanne?

00:24:55.195 --> 00:24:56.235
Just try what?

00:24:57.435 --> 00:25:00.955
>> I was gonna say the phone, but then you don't have the visual, never mind. Yep.

00:25:01.355 --> 00:25:17.570
So I think we're doing as good as we can there at this point. Yeah. Jed, I have another comment. Yep. The property owner can submit a special permit. It's really semantics, but I mean, submit an application or apply for a special permit. Submit an application for a special permit.

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:18.610
This is

00:25:27.135 --> 00:25:30.335
>> There we go. That one's done deal. Okay. Thank you.

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.710
Now we get down to where we're actually talking about

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:38.630
the regulations in the overlay zone.

00:25:44.630 --> 00:25:45.430
And so

00:25:45.830 --> 00:25:46.230
of the,

00:25:47.855 --> 00:25:53.935
So we're expecting a 10% reduction in nutrients running off into the lake when all is said and done.

00:25:54.255 --> 00:25:55.375
And then we throw

00:25:55.615 --> 00:25:57.455
additionally the site plan

00:25:57.695 --> 00:26:04.010
must demonstrate that it will infiltrate the first two inch flush of storm water or volume of two inch rain event.

00:26:04.330 --> 00:26:06.810
So that's on top of that 10% reduction.

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:09.370
And

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:12.490
I said, well, maybe we're asking for too much.

00:26:13.945 --> 00:26:14.825
And then

00:26:15.945 --> 00:26:19.785
I said to myself, well, one of the feedback things I did get

00:26:20.665 --> 00:26:23.625
from Hillary was she thought she

00:26:23.625 --> 00:26:26.745
was hoping that we would have used a little more

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:30.980
technically

00:26:31.540 --> 00:26:32.500
refined

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:34.660
and accurate

00:26:38.660 --> 00:26:39.700
reduction technique.

00:26:40.145 --> 00:26:42.945
She thought it was overly simplified.

00:26:43.825 --> 00:26:46.465
And I agree that it is overly simplified,

00:26:46.625 --> 00:26:48.705
but we probably need to

00:26:49.185 --> 00:26:50.545
keep it simple

00:26:52.225 --> 00:26:58.380
because even then, it takes a while to get the handle of it. And I didn't want to make it too difficult.

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:01.100
But one of the things that

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:03.260
certainly comes into

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:04.620
play is

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:05.900
our

00:27:06.140 --> 00:27:07.100
regulations

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:09.420
do not account for the slope of the property

00:27:09.875 --> 00:27:11.635
running down into the lake.

00:27:12.035 --> 00:27:15.875
And a lot of the property has some very significant slopes

00:27:16.115 --> 00:27:21.075
and so all the water is gonna run off into the lake. Very little has a chance to

00:27:21.555 --> 00:27:23.475
soak in unless we do something there. So

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:27.740
anyhow, I decided

00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:36.860
that my recommendation is we're going leave that extra two inch flush in there. If it gets to be too cumbersome and everybody's saying they can't do it,

00:27:39.735 --> 00:27:42.215
then we'll have to look at modifying the regulations.

00:27:42.215 --> 00:27:45.175
But I think we should try to go with

00:27:45.495 --> 00:27:47.975
a little bit more and see

00:27:48.295 --> 00:27:49.335
how we do there.

00:27:50.055 --> 00:27:54.375
This is where Hillary made the comment, if the homeowners went through the trouble of

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:59.200
hiring someone to create best management practices to infiltrate

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:02.960
this water, they should be encouraged to infiltrate runoff

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:07.040
from existing structures. And I agreed and that's why I put that comment

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:10.240
early on in the intent about doing that.

00:28:13.975 --> 00:28:15.255
But this is where

00:28:16.295 --> 00:28:19.735
the actual 10% reduction for zone A

00:28:21.335 --> 00:28:25.080
is our requirement with the two inch flush for the additional

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:27.240
impervious

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:28.200
sections.

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:31.320
And so

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.840
after that, I'm gonna go down to Zone B,

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:39.800
which is the same as Zone A with the exception of

00:28:40.255 --> 00:28:42.495
instead of a 100 square feet

00:28:42.735 --> 00:28:43.615
increase,

00:28:44.095 --> 00:28:50.255
it would be greater than 200 square feet. So we're a little further away from the lake, a little less restrictive,

00:28:50.895 --> 00:28:54.735
and instead of a 10% reduction, we're looking at a 7.5%

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:55.920
reduction

00:28:56.240 --> 00:29:00.480
on the nutrient runoff. Then we still have that two inch infiltration

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:01.520
of water.

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:08.480
Zone C, very similar.

00:29:10.235 --> 00:29:12.075
Instead of 100

00:29:12.075 --> 00:29:14.795
or 200, now it's 300 square feet.

00:29:15.115 --> 00:29:17.515
Instead of 10 or 7.5%,

00:29:17.515 --> 00:29:20.795
now it's down to a five, we're expecting a 5% reduction.

00:29:22.235 --> 00:29:23.195
So that's

00:29:23.515 --> 00:29:28.310
what all this goes through. Then we get into overlay zone D.

00:29:28.870 --> 00:29:33.110
And you don't have to do the nutrient allocation worksheet. We're just going to look for

00:29:33.350 --> 00:29:35.510
a mitigation of that two inch

00:29:35.910 --> 00:29:38.230
flush of storm water off the newly

00:29:38.630 --> 00:29:39.670
impervious

00:29:41.245 --> 00:29:42.285
surfaces.

00:29:47.965 --> 00:29:53.805
Modifications to the nutrient allocation worksheet. So this just says that if you're doing

00:29:54.365 --> 00:29:55.565
something

00:29:56.620 --> 00:30:00.220
and we don't have on our worksheet a coefficient

00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:01.580
for that improvement

00:30:01.580 --> 00:30:03.340
to reduce the runoff,

00:30:06.380 --> 00:30:12.140
you can have your engineer propose one. Jim agrees to it, we can use it, we might include it on our list.

00:30:12.675 --> 00:30:14.355
But if you recall last

00:30:14.675 --> 00:30:16.275
time when we were going through,

00:30:16.595 --> 00:30:17.635
I think we took,

00:30:19.315 --> 00:30:25.715
we were talking about how did we get the reduction coefficient when we took roof to a dry well.

00:30:26.750 --> 00:30:28.750
And if I had taken

00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:30.910
roof to lawn,

00:30:31.310 --> 00:30:37.950
it would be you take the runoff for roof, which I think is one point zero, and then lawn, which is 0.3,

00:30:37.950 --> 00:30:39.790
and you'd get the reduction of 0.7.

00:30:40.485 --> 00:30:45.125
That's how that worked and that's how it should have worked. There wasn't any reduction for

00:30:45.365 --> 00:30:47.685
a dry well. I think it could have been zero.

00:30:48.805 --> 00:30:51.845
But there were things on there we didn't have coefficients for.

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:59.770
And so I think by good engineering practice, they were developed over in Columbia and we just continued

00:30:59.770 --> 00:31:00.570
to use those here.

00:31:05.695 --> 00:31:08.495
Mitigation of one time small surface change

00:31:08.575 --> 00:31:11.455
in the A, B and C districts.

00:31:12.255 --> 00:31:16.335
And again, if you're less than 100 square feet in zone A,

00:31:16.735 --> 00:31:23.580
200 square feet in zone B, 300 square feet in zone C, You can put a trench under the roofline and

00:31:24.140 --> 00:31:24.940
capture

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:26.220
the rain

00:31:27.180 --> 00:31:30.860
and you don't have to go through the process of nutrient allocation worksheet and all that.

00:31:33.115 --> 00:31:33.755
There

00:31:33.995 --> 00:31:35.995
was some thought and I

00:31:36.555 --> 00:31:37.675
think that

00:31:38.235 --> 00:31:40.315
the thought has some merit. If

00:31:41.355 --> 00:31:43.435
you're digging a big trench

00:31:43.915 --> 00:31:45.595
in the Wetland review area,

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:47.880
are

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:53.880
there times where you would be doing more harm than good by digging this trench in the wetlands area?

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:55.720
And that could be.

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:56.1000
And if

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:00.280
Jim is the zoning agent and

00:32:01.315 --> 00:32:08.035
Joe is the wetlands agent. We're to conclude that that was the case. They can come to us and say we really think

00:32:08.275 --> 00:32:11.955
it's best if we don't do that for this small improvement.

00:32:12.195 --> 00:32:12.755
Not

00:32:13.395 --> 00:32:13.955
worth doing.

00:32:16.780 --> 00:32:17.980
But I did like

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:19.500
having the

00:32:19.900 --> 00:32:20.540
basic

00:32:21.020 --> 00:32:22.780
trench or the stone pad,

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:28.140
know, preventment under a shed or that kind of thing and we'll

00:32:28.140 --> 00:32:29.340
keep that in there.

00:32:35.795 --> 00:32:38.835
And I talked, once again, we talk about

00:32:40.355 --> 00:32:42.835
recording in the property records,

00:32:44.810 --> 00:32:47.610
the form contained in Appendix four, that

00:32:47.850 --> 00:32:49.370
covenants and restrictions

00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:53.850
form, which we don't have, and I'm just going to go ahead and eliminate that.

00:32:58.545 --> 00:33:03.505
Now we get into a section that talks about the best management practice for reduction of nitrogen,

00:33:05.185 --> 00:33:07.265
and that's just good

00:33:07.345 --> 00:33:08.385
things to do.

00:33:09.265 --> 00:33:09.665
Again,

00:33:10.850 --> 00:33:15.010
don't think anybody's going to be looking in our regs to figure out

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:17.490
how to do that. It could happen,

00:33:18.050 --> 00:33:19.170
it gives us

00:33:19.890 --> 00:33:22.050
ideas of things that we can talk

00:33:22.770 --> 00:33:26.905
to the property owner about when he's coming to us for an application

00:33:27.865 --> 00:33:28.425
and

00:33:28.745 --> 00:33:32.025
say, could you consider doing some of these things here?

00:33:37.705 --> 00:33:38.265
Down here,

00:33:39.990 --> 00:33:45.590
we had something maintain a mulched area, especially on the downslope of lawns and hard surfaces,

00:33:46.070 --> 00:33:48.470
especially at the edge above the shoreline.

00:33:51.110 --> 00:33:53.190
I thought, okay, I can do that.

00:33:53.945 --> 00:33:55.705
But

00:33:55.705 --> 00:33:56.425
I'm not

00:33:56.745 --> 00:33:57.385
sure

00:33:58.425 --> 00:34:00.265
if you put the mulch in there,

00:34:01.465 --> 00:34:02.985
does that add nutrient

00:34:03.145 --> 00:34:03.785
to

00:34:04.905 --> 00:34:08.640
the lake? I used to have a leaf pile down by the shore and

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:10.960
I dumped all my leaves there in the fall.

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:19.680
And I stopped doing that because I thought that would increase the nutrient running off into the lake. It certainly prevents the water running off into the lake because it gets absorbed

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:22.080
into the leaf pile. But

00:34:22.505 --> 00:34:24.105
based on that's what we had,

00:34:24.585 --> 00:34:26.265
I left it there and

00:34:26.825 --> 00:34:27.945
I'll

00:34:27.945 --> 00:34:28.585
still

00:34:31.065 --> 00:34:34.905
count on my ability to get smarter as we go along

00:34:34.900 --> 00:34:39.300
and figure out best how to take care of the lake. Not an expert on that yet. But

00:34:40.020 --> 00:34:45.300
that was the question on this. It also talks about planting shrubs along the lake,

00:34:45.700 --> 00:34:47.220
and I think that's

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:48.820
a good thing to do too.

00:34:54.105 --> 00:34:56.745
Okay, so this is just modification

00:34:57.945 --> 00:35:01.305
of lake watershed protection requirements by special permit.

00:35:03.590 --> 00:35:05.350
And this is where it talks about

00:35:06.150 --> 00:35:07.670
and Jim, see if

00:35:08.390 --> 00:35:12.150
we got the words that you like here in a case where

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:13.910
unable to comply.

00:35:18.675 --> 00:35:25.395
Okay, so it sort of avoids the issue. It just says the Commission may modify the requirements by special permit. So I guess that's

00:35:26.595 --> 00:35:28.990
fine with the way that's worded there. And

00:35:29.550 --> 00:35:33.310
that's what I tied back to that earlier process just so that

00:35:35.390 --> 00:35:42.910
you knew as you were looking through the regs if there was a problem and you couldn't meet it, we had an out for you on how to go.

00:35:53.675 --> 00:35:54.875
This is a

